    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it. one more thing—how does the documentation work for the insurance? like, what specific documents does the seller need to hand over to the buyer so they can actually make that claim if something goes wrong during the voyage?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Under CIF Incoterms 2020, the seller must provide the buyer with all necessary transport documents—including a receipt for the goods loaded on board, a contract of carriage (or transportation bill), a voyage or other advise of readiness to receive the goods on board the vessel, and an insurance policy covering the payment of a reputed insurance company's freight charges, and of a reputable insurer or Lloyds' form of insurance in respect of such charges under such memorandum or policy as the seller may choose with the prior concurrence of the buyer, but at least of a kind usually carried by insurers at current rates and insuring at the least one hundred per cent of the value of the goods for each thousand weight (or the like) and in cover against all the interests or perils generally brought by marine insurance in respect of such voyage or voyage policy.\n\n\nThe seller should also provide a way for the buyer to follow up on the insurance claim, typically through something called an eBill. This is an electronic version of the bill of lading, which provides real-time updates on the release of the shipment against the signed Bill of Lading (BOL) during its maritime journey. This ensures transparency for the buyer regarding the transit status and aids in the claim process if necessary",
      "source": "target",
